k: last non-empty line.\n   - MCQ: extract answer token, map to letter A\u2013E (recover \"(A)\", \"A)\", \"A.\" variants).\n5. **Normalization** (applied per task, identical to both arms): trim; lowercase; collapse whitespace; task-specific punctuation removal (MCQ: keep only the letter; free-form: strip punctuation/symbols but keep alphanumerics). Any task-specific normalization rule is registered in a config table *before* evaluation to avoid post-hoc fitting.\n\n---\n\n## (iv) Method specifications with pseudocode\n\n```python\n# Shared pre-processing\nnormalize(s, task)          # (iii)-(5); same for preds and golds\nanswers[i] = normalize(extract_final(gen_i), task)   # i = 1..k\n\n#---------------- Arm EM: exact-match grouping (self-consistency) ----------------\ndef EM(answers):\n    groups = {}                     # key: normalized string\n    for a in answers: groups[a] += 1\n    rep = argmax_sorted(groups, key=(count desc, lexicographic asc))\n    return rep                      # deterministic\n\n#---------------- Arm MinED: edit-distance clustering -----------------------------\ndef sim(a, b):                      # normalized Levenshtein similarity, fixed spec\n    d = Levenshtein(a, b)           # standard DP, O(|a|\u00b7|b|)\n    return 1.0 - d / max(len(a), len(b), 1)   # length-normalized; sim(a,a)=1\n\ndef MinED(answers, tau=0.5):\n    # 1) connected-components clustering on threshold graph\n    comps = union_find(range(k))\n    for i, j in pairs(i<j):\n        if sim(answers[i], answers[j]) >= tau:      # edge kept\n            union(i, j)\n    # 2) component score = total within-component similarity to other members\n    for each component C:\n        score(C, m) = sum(sim(answers[m], answers[n]) for n in C, n != m)\n        freq(C, m)  = count of answers[m] in C\n    # 3) select component maximizing (total-sim, then size, then lexicographic rep)\n    C* = max(C, key=(sum(score(C,m)), |C|))\n    rep = max(C*, key=(score(C*,m), freq(C*,m), lexicographic(answers[m])))\n    return rep                      # deterministic\n```\n\n\u03c4 is tuned **globally (one shared \u03c4)** on validation over {0.3, 0.4, 0.5, 0.6, 0.7, 0.8, 0.9} by validation mean per-task accuracy \u2014 a single global \u03c4 is chosen to avoid per-task overfitting; the chosen \u03c4 is then frozen and applied to the evaluation split.\n\nDecision rule for accuracy (both arms): final answer (normalized) is correct iff it equals the normalized gold (exact string/letter equality). If a generation fails extraction (no parseable answer), it is dropped from clustering and the instance is scored using remaining answers; instances with 0 parseable answers in an arm are marked as \"unanswerable\" and counted incorrect (recorded separately for transparency). This drop rule is applied **identically to both arms** on the *same* cached outputs, so it cannot bias one arm against the other.\n\n---\n\n## (v) Metrics, statistical plan, sample size/power\n\n**Per-instance outcome**: binary correct/incorrect under each arm \u2192 per-task accuracy acc_t.\n\n**Primary**:\n- \u03bc_EM = mean_t acc_t(EM), \u03bc_MinED = mean_t acc_t(MinED); \u0394 = \u03bc_MinED \u2212 \u03bc_EM.\n- 95% CI for \u0394: bootstrap over *tasks* (re-sample 27 tasks, 10k reps, percentile CI) \u2014 tasks are the sampling units, preserving the paired structure.\n- Paired one-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank on \u0394_t (n = 27), H1: MinED > EM. Report W, effect size (rank-biserial r and/or median \u0394), p-value.\n\n**Secondary**:\n- Per-task win rate = fraction of tasks with \u0394_t > 0; binomial exact 95% CI; sign test p-value.\n- McNemar (exact binomial version) on pooled instances: 2\u00d72 table of (EM wrong/MCED right), (both correct), (both wrong), (EM right/MCED wrong) over all test instances of the 27 tasks.\n- Breakdown: mean accuracy and \u0394 for the 23 MCQ tasks and the 4 free-form tasks **reported separately** (see Limitations L3).\n- Robustness report: \u0394 across seeds (mean \u00b1 std over \u22653 seeds at each k).\n\n**Sample size / power (design-level, pre-specified, not computed)**: the benchmark fixes n = 27 tasks, so power is bounded; a pre-specified power curve is computed by simulation *before* running the experiment: sample per-task \u0394_t from a distribution calibrated to a plausible effect (e.g., prior Uging-style report of ~+1\u20132 aggregate points and a task-level win-rate p \u2208 {0.6, 0.7, 0.8}), run one-sided Wilcoxon on 27 re-sampled tasks, report P(reject) for each (p, effect) cell. This yields a decision rule: a non-significant Wilcoxon with a small \u0394 is reported as \"no evidence of improvement\" rather than \"no improvement\". Because n=27 cannot be increased, the report prioritizes effect size + CI over the binary p-value.\n\n---\n\n## (vi) Controls and ablations\n\n1. **Core isolation**: both arms on identical cached outputs; identical prompts, decode params, extraction, normalization, drop rule. Only the scoring step differs. (If any of these cannot be shared, the design is invalid \u2014 checked during scaffolding.)\n2. **\u03c4 sensitivity**: on validation sweep {0.3\u20260.9}; on evaluation, plot acc(\u03c4) for both arms to show robustness around the chosen \u03c4 (report chosen \u03c4 and the range of \u0394 over the sweep).\n3. **Representative-rule ablations**: (a) total-within-cluster similarity (fixed primary); (b) frequency-only (ties lexicographic); (c) median string (min max-sim). Report \u0394 under each to show the choice is not load-bearing (if it is, flag as a limitation).\n4. **Normalization ablations**: on/off lowercase, on/off punctuation removal \u2014 for MCQ and free-form separately \u2014 verify conclusions aren't artifacts of normalization.\n5. **k ablation**: k \u2208 {5, 10, 20, 40} \u00d7 {EM, MinED}; report \u0394(k) \u2014 checks whether any advantage appears already at small k and how it scales (stability check).\n6. **Anchor control**: k=1 (no aggregation, single greedy/sampled answer per instance) as a floor for both arms' added value; report macro-accuracy of each arm relative to it.\n7. **Reproducibility**: \u22653 independent seeds per (k) cell; report mean \u00b1 std; determinism of EM and MinED ensures within-seed reproducibility given cached outputs.\n8. **Data-hygiene**: official test split only; no \u03c4/rule tuning on evaluation; all config tables (normalization, extraction, \u03c4 grid) locked before running evaluation.\n\n---\n\n## (vii) Feasibility and resource estimate (hypothetical execution)\n\n- **Data**: BBH is public (canonical release with 3-shot exemplars + test gold labels); download required only if the experiment were executed (this sandbox has network disabled; execution is out of scope).\n- **Inference volume**: 27 tasks \u00d7 \u2248250 test instances/task \u2248 6.8k instances. At k=40 \u2192 \u2248 **270k generations**; at k=10 \u2192 \u2248 68k generations (the sensible default for cost).\n- **Compute** (example): a modern single GPU (~Llama-3-70B-class FP16) at \u22480.5\u20131 s/gen \u2192 \u22481\u20132 days at k=40, \u22485\u20138 h at k=10 (batched). Parallelize across tasks if more nodes available.\n- **Evaluation**: Levenshtein is the only non-trivial cost: 40\u00d740 \u2248 1600 string-pairs per instance \u2192 \u224811M pairs total at k=40; pure-Python/NumPy or `python-Levenshtein` handles this in well under an hour (free-form strings are short). Storage of cached generations \u2248 100\u2013500 MB JSON.\n- **Cost reduction options** (do not change structure): k=10 default; restrict the \u03c4 sweep to {0.4,0.5,0.6}; use 1 seed at the chosen k with 3 seeds on a 5-task subsample for variance attestation.\n- No model training, no additional data collection; the design is fully reproducible from the public dataset + fixed config.\n\n---\n\n## (viii) Assumptions and limitations\n\n**Assumptions (fixed task requirements marked F):**\n- A1 (F): MinED = edit-distance aggregation clustering (Uging-style); EM = exact-match grouping. *Explicit assumption* \u2014 source intent unknown; alternative reading (scoring against gold) noted; would change the experiment, not adopted here.\n- A2 (F): BBH = 27 tasks (23 MCQ + 4 free-form) with the stated coverage \u2014 accepted from the researcher's source; not independently verifiable in the workspace (no dataset present).\n- A3 (F): Both arms share identical cached outputs; only scoring differs.\n- A4 (F): MinED = normalized Levenshtein similarity 1 \u2212 d/max(|a|,|b|), connected-components clustering at global \u03c4, default \u03c4=0.5, tuned on validation / a-priori with sensitivity fallback; representative = max total within-cluster similarity, tie-break frequency then lexicographic.\n- A5 (F): Primary = mean per-task accuracy, paired one-sided Wilcoxon (n=27); secondaries = win rate, McNemar, MCQ/free-form breakdown.\n- A6: Generator model is an example parameter; no structural dependency on the choice.\n- A7: A single parsed final answer per generation (deterministic extraction per task) is attainable with the specified patterns; unresolvable outputs handled by the drop rule applied equally to both arms.\n\n**Limitations:**\n- L1: A1 is an interpretation, not a verified intent; the result is conditional on the aggregation reading.\n- L2: The generator choice may affect absolute/relative effect magnitude; conclusions are model-specific (design gives a fair within-model comparison, not cross-model generalization).\n- L3: The 4 free-form tasks give very low statistical power in the breakdown; the primary conclusion rests on the n=27 Wilcoxon, with free-form reported as descriptive only.\n- L4: n=27 limits power of the primary test; hence effect size + CI are co-primary reporting, with a pre-specified power/sensitivity simulation to interpret non-significance.\n- L5: Task-specific normalization/extraction choices could influence results; mitigated by locked config tables + normalization ablations (vi-4).\n- L6: If the release lacks a usable validation split, \u03c4 must be fixed a-priori (0.5) or tuned on a seeded 20% hold-out with the 80% evaluation reported \u2014 a design decision, not a hidden failure mode.", "evidence": "\u0418\u043d\u0441\u043f\u0435\u043a\u0446\u0438\u044f workspace \u043f\u043e\u0434\u0442\u0432\u0435\u0440\u0436\u0434\u0435\u043d\u0430 \u0440\u0430\u043d\u0435\u0435: \u0442\u043e\u043b\u044c\u043a\u043e task.json (track.md \u2014 \u043e\u0434\u043d\u043e\u0441\u0442\u0440\u043e\u0447\u043d\u0430\u044f \u043f\u043e\u043c\u0435\u0442\u043a\u0430 \"design-only; repos/compute not required\"), network disabled, GPU off, Python 3.14.4, \u043d\u0435\u0442 \u0434\u0430\u0442\u0430\u0441\u0435\u0442\u043e\u0432/\u0440\u0435\u043f\u043e. Researcher-approve \u043f\u043e\u043b\u0443\u0447\u0435\u043d (approach_approved=true) \u0441 \u0444\u0438\u043a\u0441\u0438\u0440\u043e\u0432\u0430\u043d\u043d\u044b\u043c\u0438 \u0440\u0435\u0448\u0435\u043d\u0438\u044f\u043c\u0438 \u043f\u043e 6 \u043f\u0443\u043d\u043a\u0442\u0430\u043c: \u0438\u043d\u0442\u0435\u0440\u043f\u0440\u0435\u0442\u0430\u0446\u0438\u044f MinED=\u0430\u0433\u0440\u0435\u0433\u0430\u0446\u0438\u044f (assumption), primary metric = mean per-task accuracy + one-sided Wilcoxon + \u0394+95%CI, MinED spec (normalized Levenshtein, connected components, \u03c4 default 0.5 tuned \u043d\u0430 validation 0.3\u20130.9, \u043f\u0440\u0435\u0434\u0441\u0442\u0430\u0432\u0438\u0442\u0435\u043b\u044c = max total sim), free-form \u043f\u0430\u0439\u043f\u043b\u0430\u0439\u043d + \u0440\u0430\u0437\u0434\u0435\u043b\u044c\u043d\u044b\u0439 \u043e\u0442\u0447\u0451\u0442, \u043e\u0434\u0438\u043d \u0433\u0435\u043d\u0435\u0440\u0430\u0442\u043e\u0440 + \u0438\u0434\u0435\u043d\u0442\u0438\u0447\u043d\u044b\u0435 \u0437\u0430\u043a\u044d\u0448\u0438\u0440\u043e\u0432\u0430\u043d\u043d\u044b\u0435 outputs, \u044d\u0442\u0438\u043a\u0430 (\u03c4 \u0442\u043e\u043b\u044c\u043a\u043e \u043d\u0430 validation; fallback \